{x+1}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply both sides of the equation by $(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)$ to clear the fractions:\n\n$$\n2x^2+5x+7= A(x-3)(x+1)+B(x+2)(x+1)+C(x+2)(x-3)\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2$ gets rid of the $B$ and $C$ terms and allows us to solve for $A$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(-2)^{2} + 5(-2) + 7 &= A(-2 - 3)(-2 + 1) \\\\\n5 &= A(5) \\\\\nA &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3$ gets rid of the $A$ and $C$ terms and allows us to solve for $B$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(3)^{2} + 5(3) + 7 &= B(3 + 2)(3 + 1) \\\\\n40 &= B(20) \\\\\nB &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, plugging in $x=-1$ gets rid of the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(-1)^{2} + 5(-1) + 7 &= C(-1 + 2)(-1 - 3) \\\\\n4 &= C(-4) \\\\\nC &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing that $A=1$, $B=2$, and $C=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+5x+7}{(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} +\\dfrac{2}{x-3} -\\dfrac{1}{x+1}\n$$" 13194139534620 false] [17592186096410 1145 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+5x+7}{(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{x+2} + \\dfrac{B}{x-3} + \\dfrac{C}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply both sides of the equation by $(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)$ to clear the fractions:\n\n$$\n2x^2+5x+7= A(x-3)(x+1)+B(x+2)(x+1)+C(x+2)(x-3)\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2$ gets rid of the $B$ and $C$ terms and allows us to solve for $A.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)^{2}+5(-2)+7 & =A(-2-3)(-2+1) \\\\ 5 & =A(5) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3$ gets rid of the $A$ and $C$ terms and allows us to solve for $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)^{2}+5(3)+7 & =B(3+2)(3+1) \\\\ 40 & =B(20) \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, plugging in $x=-1$ gets rid of the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)^{2}+5(-1)+7 & =C(-1+2)(-1-3) \\\\ 4 & =C(-4) \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing that $A=1,$ $B=2,$ and $C=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+5x+7}{(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} +\\dfrac{2}{x-3} -\\dfrac{1}{x+1}.\n$$" 13194139534620 true] [17592186096603 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299283 13194139534620 true] [17592186096603 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299286 13194139534620 true] [17592186096604 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299292 13194139534620 true] [17592186096604 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299299 13194139534620 true] [17592186096864 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534620 true] [17592186096864 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nExpress $(16x^{2} + 55x + 19)/((2x + 1)(x + 5)(x + 1))$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139534620 false] [17592186096864 1100 "Express $\\frac{16{x}^{2}+55x+19}{(2x+1)(x+5)(x+1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139534620 true] [17592186096864 1145 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16{x}^{2}+55x+19}{(2x+1)(x+5)(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+5)}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(2x+1)(x+5)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+55x+19=A(x+5)(x+1)+B(2x+1)(x+1)+C(2x+1)(x+5).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+55(-\\frac{1}{2})+19 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}+5)(-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & +B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1)(-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & +C(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1)(-\\frac{1}{2}+5) \\\\ 4-\\frac{55}{2}+19 & =A(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{46-55}{2} & =A(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ A & =\\frac{-36}{18} \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16(-5)^{2}+55(-5)+19 & =A(-5+5)(-5+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(-5)+1)(-5+1) \\\\  & +C(2(-5)+1)(-5+5) \\\\ 400-275+19 & =B((-9)(-4)) \\\\ B & =\\frac{144}{36} \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16(-1)^{2}+55(-1)+19 & =A(-1+5)(-1+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(-1)+1)(-1+1) \\\\  & +C(2(-1)+1)(-1+5) \\\\ 16-55+19 & =C(-4) \\\\ C & =\\frac{-20}{-4} \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{16{x}^{2}+55x+19}{(2x+1)(x+5)(x+1)}=-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{4}{(x+5)}+\\frac{5}{(x+1)}.\n$$" 13194139534620 true] [17592186096865 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534620 true] [17592186096865 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nExpress $(-2x^{2} + 21x + 41)/((x + 1)(2 - x)(x + 3))$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139534620 false] [17592186096865 1100 "Express $\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+21x+41}{(x+1)(2-x)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139534620 true] [17592186096865 1145 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+21x+41}{(x+1)(2-x)(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{(x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x+1)(2-x)(x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+21x+41=A(2-x)(x+3)+B(x+1)(x+3)+C(x+1)(2-x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-1)^{2}+21(-1)+41 & =A(2-(-1))(-1+3) \\\\  & \\,+B(-1+1)(-1+3) \\\\  & +C(-1+1)(2-(-1)) \\\\ -2-21+41 & =A(6) \\\\ A & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(2)^{2}+21(2)+41 & =A(2-2)(2+3) \\\\  & \\,+B(2+1)(2+3) \\\\  & +C(2+1)(2-2) \\\\ -8+42+41 & =B(15) \\\\ B & =\\frac{75}{15} \\\\ B & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-3)^{2}+21(-3)+41 & =A(2-(-3))(-3+3) \\\\  & \\,+B(-3+1)(-3+3) \\\\  & +C(-3+1)(2-(-3)) \\\\ -18-63+41 & =C(-10) \\\\ C & =\\frac{-40}{-10} \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+21x+41}{(x+1)(2-x)(x+3)}=\\frac{3}{(x+1)}+\\frac{5}{(2-x)}+\\frac{4}{(x+3)}.\n$$" 13194139534620 true] [17592186299283 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299284 13194139534620 true] [17592186299283 1089 "q-350934" 13194139534620 true] [17592186299283 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534620 true] [17592186299283 1100 "Express $\\frac{5x+11}{(x+1)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5x+11}{(x+1)(x+3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x+1$ |  | $x+3$ |" 13194139534620 true] [17592186299283 1113 #uuid "d5c75d54-5a8a-5074-ad31